UPDATE public.questions SET stimulus = $hdsat_report_fix$Kelp forests are underwater areas with a high density of kelp, which covers a large part of the world's coastlines. Smaller areas of anchored kelp are called kelp beds. They are recognized as one of the most productive and dynamic ecosystems on Earth. Although algal kelp forest combined with coral reefs only cover 0.1% of Earth's total surface, they account for 0.9% of global primary productivity. Kelp forests occur worldwide throughout temperate and polar coastal oceans. In 2007, kelp forests were also discovered in tropical waters near Ecuador.

______$hdsat_report_fix$, prompt = $hdsat_report_fix$Which choice most logically completes the text?$hdsat_report_fix$, choices = $hdsat_report_fix$[{"id":"A","text":"Expand only when ocean temperatures are falling."},{"id":"B","text":"Grow in waters with very different temperature conditions."},{"id":"C","text":"Survive only where coral reefs are also present."},{"id":"D","text":"Thrive best in the warm waters near Ecuador."}]$hdsat_report_fix$::jsonb, figure_metadata = NULL, skill = $hdsat_report_fix$Inferences$hdsat_report_fix$, domain = $hdsat_report_fix$Information and Ideas$hdsat_report_fix$, difficulty = $hdsat_report_fix$Hard$hdsat_report_fix$, format = $hdsat_report_fix$multiple_choice$hdsat_report_fix$, assessments = ARRAY[$hdsat_report_fix$SAT$hdsat_report_fix$, $hdsat_report_fix$PSAT/NMSQT$hdsat_report_fix$, $hdsat_report_fix$PSAT 10$hdsat_report_fix$, $hdsat_report_fix$PSAT 8/9$hdsat_report_fix$]::TEXT[], updated_at = NOW() WHERE id = $hdsat_report_fix$RW-M16-INF001$hdsat_report_fix$;
UPDATE public.question_keys SET correct_answer = $hdsat_report_fix$B$hdsat_report_fix$, acceptable_answers = ARRAY[$hdsat_report_fix$B$hdsat_report_fix$]::TEXT[], explanation = $hdsat_report_fix$The passage places kelp forests in temperate, polar and, since 2007, tropical waters. A range spanning such different climates supports the inference that the forests tolerate a broad range of temperatures.$hdsat_report_fix$, distractor_rationales = $hdsat_report_fix${"C":"The text never ties kelp forests to the presence of coral reefs.","D":"The Ecuador discovery shows kelp forests can grow in tropical water; it does not show that they do best there.","A":"Nothing in the text links kelp expansion to falling temperatures."}$hdsat_report_fix$::jsonb, updated_at = NOW() WHERE question_id = $hdsat_report_fix$RW-M16-INF001$hdsat_report_fix$;
UPDATE public.questions SET stimulus = $hdsat_report_fix$Roman concrete, also called opus caementicium, was used in construction in ancient Rome. Like its modern equivalent, Roman concrete was based on a hydraulic-setting cement added to an aggregate. Many buildings and structures still standing today, such as bridges, reservoirs and aqueducts, were built with this material, which attests to both its versatility and its durability. It was previously thought that its strength was enhanced by the incorporation of pozzolanic ash where available (particularly in the Bay of Naples). The addition of ash prevented cracks from spreading. However, research in 2023 has shown that the incorporation of mixtures of different types of lime, forming conglomerate "clasts" allowed the concrete to self-repair cracks, which was more likely the case.

______$hdsat_report_fix$, prompt = $hdsat_report_fix$Which choice most logically completes the text?$hdsat_report_fix$, choices = $hdsat_report_fix$[{"id":"A","text":"The absence of aggregate in the Roman mixture."},{"id":"B","text":"Stucco and marble applied to interior surfaces."},{"id":"C","text":"Pozzolanic ash imported from the Bay of Naples."},{"id":"D","text":"Lime clasts that could seal cracks as they formed."}]$hdsat_report_fix$::jsonb, figure_metadata = NULL, skill = $hdsat_report_fix$Inferences$hdsat_report_fix$, domain = $hdsat_report_fix$Information and Ideas$hdsat_report_fix$, difficulty = $hdsat_report_fix$Hard$hdsat_report_fix$, format = $hdsat_report_fix$multiple_choice$hdsat_report_fix$, assessments = ARRAY[$hdsat_report_fix$SAT$hdsat_report_fix$, $hdsat_report_fix$PSAT/NMSQT$hdsat_report_fix$, $hdsat_report_fix$PSAT 10$hdsat_report_fix$, $hdsat_report_fix$PSAT 8/9$hdsat_report_fix$]::TEXT[], updated_at = NOW() WHERE id = $hdsat_report_fix$RW-M16-INF002$hdsat_report_fix$;
UPDATE public.question_keys SET correct_answer = $hdsat_report_fix$D$hdsat_report_fix$, acceptable_answers = ARRAY[$hdsat_report_fix$D$hdsat_report_fix$]::TEXT[], explanation = $hdsat_report_fix$The 2023 finding is that lime clasts allowed self-repair. Completing the sentence with lime clasts follows directly from that finding; the other completions either contradict the text or name unrelated details.$hdsat_report_fix$, distractor_rationales = $hdsat_report_fix${"C":"The ash explanation is the older view that the 2023 research displaced.","A":"The text says Roman concrete was based on cement added to an aggregate, so the mixture was not aggregate-free.","B":"Stucco and marble are described as interior decoration, not as a cause of crack resistance."}$hdsat_report_fix$::jsonb, updated_at = NOW() WHERE question_id = $hdsat_report_fix$RW-M16-INF002$hdsat_report_fix$;
UPDATE public.questions SET stimulus = $hdsat_report_fix$Waggle dance is a term used in beekeeping and ethology for a particular figure-eight dance of the honey bee. By performing this dance, successful foragers can share information about the direction and distance to patches of flowers yielding nectar and pollen, to water sources, or to new nest-site locations with other members of the colony. The waggle dance and the round dance are two forms of dance behaviour that are part of a continuous transition. As the distance between the resource and the hive increases, the round dance transforms into variations of a transitional dance, which, when communicating resources at even greater distances, becomes the waggle dance. In the case of Apis mellifera ligustica, the round dance is performed until the resource is about ten metres (33 ft) away from the hive, transitional dances are performed when the resource is at a distance of twenty to thirty metres (66 to 98 ft) away from the hive, and finally, when it is located at distances greater than forty metres (130 ft) from the hive, the waggle dance is performed.

______$hdsat_report_fix$, prompt = $hdsat_report_fix$Which choice most logically completes the text?$hdsat_report_fix$, choices = $hdsat_report_fix$[{"id":"A","text":"Perform the full waggle dance because the resource is beyond forty metres."},{"id":"B","text":"Perform a transitional dance rather than either the round dance or the full waggle dance."},{"id":"C","text":"Abandon dancing and lead the colony directly to the resource."},{"id":"D","text":"Perform the round dance until the resource is ten metres away."}]$hdsat_report_fix$::jsonb, figure_metadata = NULL, skill = $hdsat_report_fix$Inferences$hdsat_report_fix$, domain = $hdsat_report_fix$Information and Ideas$hdsat_report_fix$, difficulty = $hdsat_report_fix$Hard$hdsat_report_fix$, format = $hdsat_report_fix$multiple_choice$hdsat_report_fix$, assessments = ARRAY[$hdsat_report_fix$SAT$hdsat_report_fix$, $hdsat_report_fix$PSAT/NMSQT$hdsat_report_fix$, $hdsat_report_fix$PSAT 10$hdsat_report_fix$, $hdsat_report_fix$PSAT 8/9$hdsat_report_fix$]::TEXT[], updated_at = NOW() WHERE id = $hdsat_report_fix$RW-M16-INF003$hdsat_report_fix$;
UPDATE public.question_keys SET correct_answer = $hdsat_report_fix$B$hdsat_report_fix$, acceptable_answers = ARRAY[$hdsat_report_fix$B$hdsat_report_fix$]::TEXT[], explanation = $hdsat_report_fix$In Apis mellifera ligustica the transitional dances occur at twenty to thirty metres, so a forager returning from twenty-five metres would perform a transitional dance.$hdsat_report_fix$, distractor_rationales = $hdsat_report_fix${"D":"Ten metres marks the upper end of the round dance, not a resource twenty-five metres away.","A":"Forty metres is the threshold for the full waggle dance; twenty-five metres falls below it.","C":"The text describes dancing as the means of sharing information and never mentions leading by flight."}$hdsat_report_fix$::jsonb, updated_at = NOW() WHERE question_id = $hdsat_report_fix$RW-M16-INF003$hdsat_report_fix$;
UPDATE public.questions SET stimulus = $hdsat_report_fix$A printing press is a machine that applies pressure to transfer ink onto a surface made of paper, cloth, or a similar material. Its workers are known as printing press operators. It marked a significant improvement on earlier methods, in which the paper was rubbed by hand to transfer applied ink to the printing surface. The printing press was invented by the German goldsmith Johannes Gutenberg around 1440, and its subsequent global spread revolutionized literary production and communication during the early modern period. The spread of printing brought about an era of mass communication in European society, allowing information to quickly spread across Europe and contributing to the Reformation and the Scientific Revolution. The cost of printed texts declined and their availability increased, facilitating higher adult literacy rates across Europe. Books increasingly appeared in vernacular languages rather than Latin, and national languages were standardized.

______$hdsat_report_fix$, prompt = $hdsat_report_fix$Which choice most logically completes the text?$hdsat_report_fix$, choices = $hdsat_report_fix$[{"id":"A","text":"The invention of the wooden handpress."},{"id":"B","text":"A rise in the price of printed texts."},{"id":"C","text":"The decline of Latin as the dominant language of European books."},{"id":"D","text":"The disappearance of national languages."}]$hdsat_report_fix$::jsonb, figure_metadata = NULL, skill = $hdsat_report_fix$Inferences$hdsat_report_fix$, domain = $hdsat_report_fix$Information and Ideas$hdsat_report_fix$, difficulty = $hdsat_report_fix$Medium$hdsat_report_fix$, format = $hdsat_report_fix$multiple_choice$hdsat_report_fix$, assessments = ARRAY[$hdsat_report_fix$SAT$hdsat_report_fix$, $hdsat_report_fix$PSAT/NMSQT$hdsat_report_fix$, $hdsat_report_fix$PSAT 10$hdsat_report_fix$, $hdsat_report_fix$PSAT 8/9$hdsat_report_fix$]::TEXT[], updated_at = NOW() WHERE id = $hdsat_report_fix$RW-M16-INF004$hdsat_report_fix$;
UPDATE public.question_keys SET correct_answer = $hdsat_report_fix$C$hdsat_report_fix$, acceptable_answers = ARRAY[$hdsat_report_fix$C$hdsat_report_fix$]::TEXT[], explanation = $hdsat_report_fix$The text states that books increasingly appeared in vernacular languages rather than Latin, which supports the inference that Latin's dominance in printed books declined.$hdsat_report_fix$, distractor_rationales = $hdsat_report_fix${"A":"The handpress is the machine itself, not a consequence of vernacular printing.","B":"The text reports that the cost of printed texts declined.","D":"The text says national languages were standardized, not that they disappeared."}$hdsat_report_fix$::jsonb, updated_at = NOW() WHERE question_id = $hdsat_report_fix$RW-M16-INF004$hdsat_report_fix$;
UPDATE public.questions SET stimulus = $hdsat_report_fix$The urban heat island (UHI) effect is a meteorological and climatological phenomenon in which urban areas experience significantly warmer temperatures than surrounding rural areas. The temperature difference is usually larger at night than during the day, and is most apparent when winds are weak, under block conditions, noticeably during the summer and winter. The main cause of the UHI effect is from the modification of land surfaces, while waste heat generated by energy usage is a secondary contributor. Urban areas occupy about 0.5% of the Earth's land surface but host more than half of the world's population. As a population center grows, it tends to expand its area and increase its average temperature. Another key term is heat island; the term can be used to refer to any area that is relatively hotter than the surroundings, but generally refers to human-disturbed areas.

______$hdsat_report_fix$, prompt = $hdsat_report_fix$Which choice most logically completes the text?$hdsat_report_fix$, choices = $hdsat_report_fix$[{"id":"A","text":"A windy night."},{"id":"B","text":"A calm afternoon."},{"id":"C","text":"A windy afternoon."},{"id":"D","text":"A calm night."}]$hdsat_report_fix$::jsonb, figure_metadata = NULL, skill = $hdsat_report_fix$Inferences$hdsat_report_fix$, domain = $hdsat_report_fix$Information and Ideas$hdsat_report_fix$, difficulty = $hdsat_report_fix$Hard$hdsat_report_fix$, format = $hdsat_report_fix$multiple_choice$hdsat_report_fix$, assessments = ARRAY[$hdsat_report_fix$SAT$hdsat_report_fix$, $hdsat_report_fix$PSAT/NMSQT$hdsat_report_fix$, $hdsat_report_fix$PSAT 10$hdsat_report_fix$, $hdsat_report_fix$PSAT 8/9$hdsat_report_fix$]::TEXT[], updated_at = NOW() WHERE id = $hdsat_report_fix$RW-M16-INF005$hdsat_report_fix$;
UPDATE public.question_keys SET correct_answer = $hdsat_report_fix$D$hdsat_report_fix$, acceptable_answers = ARRAY[$hdsat_report_fix$D$hdsat_report_fix$]::TEXT[], explanation = $hdsat_report_fix$The text says the urban-rural difference is larger at night and most apparent when winds are weak, so a calm night combines both conditions.$hdsat_report_fix$, distractor_rationales = $hdsat_report_fix${"C":"Wind weakens the effect, and daytime differences are usually smaller.","A":"Weak winds favor the effect, but the text says the difference is usually larger at night.","B":"Calm conditions favor the effect, but the daytime difference is usually smaller."}$hdsat_report_fix$::jsonb, updated_at = NOW() WHERE question_id = $hdsat_report_fix$RW-M16-INF005$hdsat_report_fix$;
UPDATE public.questions SET stimulus = $hdsat_report_fix$The Svalbard Global Seed Vault (Norwegian: Svalbard globale frøhvelv) is a secure backup facility for the Earth's crop diversity on the Norwegian island of Spitsbergen in the remote Arctic Svalbard archipelago. The Seed Vault provides long-term storage for duplicates of seeds from around the world, conserved in gene banks. The Seed Vault is managed under terms spelled out in a tripartite agreement among the Norwegian government, the Crop Trust, and the Nordic Genetic Resource Center (NordGen). The Norwegian government entirely funded the Seed Vault's approximately 45 million kr (US\$8.8 million in 2008) construction cost. Norway and the Crop Trust pay for operational costs. Storing seeds in the vault is free to depositors.

______$hdsat_report_fix$, prompt = $hdsat_report_fix$Which choice most logically completes the text?$hdsat_report_fix$, choices = $hdsat_report_fix$[{"id":"A","text":"Let gene banks preserve backup copies without adding storage costs."},{"id":"B","text":"Earn revenue by charging depositors for storage."},{"id":"C","text":"Limit deposits to seeds produced in Norway."},{"id":"D","text":"Replace the gene banks that hold the original seeds."}]$hdsat_report_fix$::jsonb, figure_metadata = NULL, skill = $hdsat_report_fix$Inferences$hdsat_report_fix$, domain = $hdsat_report_fix$Information and Ideas$hdsat_report_fix$, difficulty = $hdsat_report_fix$Medium$hdsat_report_fix$, format = $hdsat_report_fix$multiple_choice$hdsat_report_fix$, assessments = ARRAY[$hdsat_report_fix$SAT$hdsat_report_fix$, $hdsat_report_fix$PSAT/NMSQT$hdsat_report_fix$, $hdsat_report_fix$PSAT 10$hdsat_report_fix$, $hdsat_report_fix$PSAT 8/9$hdsat_report_fix$]::TEXT[], updated_at = NOW() WHERE id = $hdsat_report_fix$RW-M16-INF006$hdsat_report_fix$;
UPDATE public.question_keys SET correct_answer = $hdsat_report_fix$A$hdsat_report_fix$, acceptable_answers = ARRAY[$hdsat_report_fix$A$hdsat_report_fix$]::TEXT[], explanation = $hdsat_report_fix$Because storage is free and the vault holds duplicates of seeds conserved elsewhere, the arrangement lets gene banks keep backups without paying for additional storage.$hdsat_report_fix$, distractor_rationales = $hdsat_report_fix${"B":"The text says storing seeds is free to depositors, so the vault does not earn revenue that way.","D":"The vault stores duplicates of seeds conserved in gene banks, which implies the gene banks continue to hold the originals.","C":"No national restriction on deposits is described."}$hdsat_report_fix$::jsonb, updated_at = NOW() WHERE question_id = $hdsat_report_fix$RW-M16-INF006$hdsat_report_fix$;
UPDATE public.questions SET stimulus = $hdsat_report_fix$The Antikythera mechanism (AN-tik-ih-THEER-ə, US also AN-ty-kih-) is an ancient Greek hand-powered orrery (model of the Solar System) built in the 2nd century BC and discovered in 1901. It is the oldest known example of an analogue computer. It could be used to predict astronomical positions and eclipses decades in advance. It could also be used to track the four-year cycle of athletic games similar to an olympiad, the cycle of the ancient Olympic Games.

______$hdsat_report_fix$, prompt = $hdsat_report_fix$Which choice most logically completes the text?$hdsat_report_fix$, choices = $hdsat_report_fix$[{"id":"A","text":"Replace written calendars throughout the Greek world."},{"id":"B","text":"Measure the weight of cargo carried by Greek ships."},{"id":"C","text":"Record the positions of the stars at one particular moment."},{"id":"D","text":"Model recurring cycles rather than perform a single calculation."}]$hdsat_report_fix$::jsonb, figure_metadata = NULL, skill = $hdsat_report_fix$Inferences$hdsat_report_fix$, domain = $hdsat_report_fix$Information and Ideas$hdsat_report_fix$, difficulty = $hdsat_report_fix$Hard$hdsat_report_fix$, format = $hdsat_report_fix$multiple_choice$hdsat_report_fix$, assessments = ARRAY[$hdsat_report_fix$SAT$hdsat_report_fix$, $hdsat_report_fix$PSAT/NMSQT$hdsat_report_fix$, $hdsat_report_fix$PSAT 10$hdsat_report_fix$, $hdsat_report_fix$PSAT 8/9$hdsat_report_fix$]::TEXT[], updated_at = NOW() WHERE id = $hdsat_report_fix$RW-M16-INF007$hdsat_report_fix$;
UPDATE public.question_keys SET correct_answer = $hdsat_report_fix$D$hdsat_report_fix$, acceptable_answers = ARRAY[$hdsat_report_fix$D$hdsat_report_fix$]::TEXT[], explanation = $hdsat_report_fix$The mechanism tracked the four-year athletic cycle and predicted astronomical positions decades ahead, both of which are recurring patterns rather than one-off calculations.$hdsat_report_fix$, distractor_rationales = $hdsat_report_fix${"B":"The text describes astronomical and athletic cycles, not cargo weight.","A":"Nothing in the text says the device replaced calendars.","C":"A device that predicts decades in advance is not limited to one moment."}$hdsat_report_fix$::jsonb, updated_at = NOW() WHERE question_id = $hdsat_report_fix$RW-M16-INF007$hdsat_report_fix$;
UPDATE public.questions SET stimulus = $hdsat_report_fix$A mycorrhizal network (also known as a common mycorrhizal network or CMN) is an underground network found in forests and other plant communities, created by the hyphae of mycorrhizal fungi joining with plant roots. This network connects individual plants together. Mycorrhizal relationships are most commonly mutualistic, with both partners benefiting, but can be commensal or parasitic, and a single partnership may change between any of the three types of symbiosis at different times. Mycorrhizal networks were discovered in 1997 by Suzanne Simard, professor of forest ecology at the University of British Columbia in Canada. Simard grew up in Canadian forests where her family had made a living as foresters for generations. Her field studies revealed that trees are linked to neighboring trees by an underground network of fungi that resembles the neural networks in the brain. In one study, Simard watched as a Douglas fir that had been injured by insects appeared to send chemical warning signals to a ponderosa pine growing nearby. The pine tree then produced defense enzymes to protect against the insect.

______$hdsat_report_fix$, prompt = $hdsat_report_fix$Which choice most logically completes the text?$hdsat_report_fix$, choices = $hdsat_report_fix$[{"id":"A","text":"A tree can produce a chemical defense."},{"id":"B","text":"insects prefer ponderosa pines to Douglas firs."},{"id":"C","text":"injured trees stop sending signals to their neighbors."},{"id":"D","text":"the fungi produce the defense enzymes themselves."}]$hdsat_report_fix$::jsonb, figure_metadata = NULL, skill = $hdsat_report_fix$Inferences$hdsat_report_fix$, domain = $hdsat_report_fix$Information and Ideas$hdsat_report_fix$, difficulty = $hdsat_report_fix$Hard$hdsat_report_fix$, format = $hdsat_report_fix$multiple_choice$hdsat_report_fix$, assessments = ARRAY[$hdsat_report_fix$SAT$hdsat_report_fix$, $hdsat_report_fix$PSAT/NMSQT$hdsat_report_fix$, $hdsat_report_fix$PSAT 10$hdsat_report_fix$, $hdsat_report_fix$PSAT 8/9$hdsat_report_fix$]::TEXT[], updated_at = NOW() WHERE id = $hdsat_report_fix$RW-M16-INF008$hdsat_report_fix$;
UPDATE public.question_keys SET correct_answer = $hdsat_report_fix$A$hdsat_report_fix$, acceptable_answers = ARRAY[$hdsat_report_fix$A$hdsat_report_fix$]::TEXT[], explanation = $hdsat_report_fix$The pine produced defense enzymes after the fir was injured and appeared to send warning signals, which supports the inference that damage to one tree can trigger a chemical defense in another.$hdsat_report_fix$, distractor_rationales = $hdsat_report_fix${"B":"The study does not compare insect preference between the two species.","D":"The text says the pine produced the enzymes, not the fungi.","C":"The injured fir appeared to send signals, which is the opposite of stopping."}$hdsat_report_fix$::jsonb, updated_at = NOW() WHERE question_id = $hdsat_report_fix$RW-M16-INF008$hdsat_report_fix$;
UPDATE public.questions SET stimulus = $hdsat_report_fix$Coral bleaching is the process where corals become white due to loss of symbiotic algae and photosynthetic pigments. This loss of pigment can be caused by various stressors, such as changes in water temperature, light, salinity, or nutrients. A bleached coral is under stress, more vulnerable to starvation and disease, and at risk of death. The leading cause of coral bleaching is rising ocean temperatures due to climate change. Take up of carbon dioxide from the atmosphere also has been acidifying the oceans and affects coral health. Large-scale bleaching events can devastate entire reef ecosystems, affecting biodiversity and the livelihoods of coastal communities that depend on healthy reefs for food, tourism, and protection from storm surges.

______$hdsat_report_fix$, prompt = $hdsat_report_fix$Which choice most logically completes the text?$hdsat_report_fix$, choices = $hdsat_report_fix$[{"id":"A","text":"storm surges become more frequent when reefs are healthy."},{"id":"B","text":"coral bleaching raises the salinity of coastal waters."},{"id":"C","text":"Reefs that support coastal fisheries may deteriorate."},{"id":"D","text":"bleached corals attract more fish than healthy corals do."}]$hdsat_report_fix$::jsonb, figure_metadata = NULL, skill = $hdsat_report_fix$Inferences$hdsat_report_fix$, domain = $hdsat_report_fix$Information and Ideas$hdsat_report_fix$, difficulty = $hdsat_report_fix$Hard$hdsat_report_fix$, format = $hdsat_report_fix$multiple_choice$hdsat_report_fix$, assessments = ARRAY[$hdsat_report_fix$SAT$hdsat_report_fix$, $hdsat_report_fix$PSAT/NMSQT$hdsat_report_fix$, $hdsat_report_fix$PSAT 10$hdsat_report_fix$, $hdsat_report_fix$PSAT 8/9$hdsat_report_fix$]::TEXT[], updated_at = NOW() WHERE id = $hdsat_report_fix$RW-M16-INF009$hdsat_report_fix$;
UPDATE public.question_keys SET correct_answer = $hdsat_report_fix$C$hdsat_report_fix$, acceptable_answers = ARRAY[$hdsat_report_fix$C$hdsat_report_fix$]::TEXT[], explanation = $hdsat_report_fix$The text says coastal communities depend on healthy reefs for food, and that bleaching can devastate reef ecosystems, which supports the inference that fisheries depending on those reefs may suffer.$hdsat_report_fix$, distractor_rationales = $hdsat_report_fix${"D":"The text links bleaching to vulnerability and death, not to attracting fish.","A":"Healthy reefs are described as protection from storm surges, not as a cause of them.","B":"Salinity is listed as a possible stressor, not as a result of bleaching."}$hdsat_report_fix$::jsonb, updated_at = NOW() WHERE question_id = $hdsat_report_fix$RW-M16-INF009$hdsat_report_fix$;
UPDATE public.questions SET stimulus = $hdsat_report_fix$The Voyager Golden Records are two identical phonograph records, with one copy aboard each of the two Voyager spacecraft launched in 1977. The records contain sounds and data to reconstruct raster scan images selected to portray the diversity of life and culture on Earth, and are intended for any intelligent extraterrestrial life form who may find them. The records are a time capsule.
Although neither Voyager spacecraft is heading toward any particular star, Voyager 1 will pass within 1.6 light-years' distance of the star Gliese 445, currently in the constellation Camelopardalis, in about 40,000 years.

______$hdsat_report_fix$, prompt = $hdsat_report_fix$Which choice most logically completes the text?$hdsat_report_fix$, choices = $hdsat_report_fix$[{"id":"A","text":"Be found and interpreted anytime soon."},{"id":"B","text":"Contain information about life on Earth."},{"id":"C","text":"Pass near the star Gliese 445."},{"id":"D","text":"Survive the journey beyond the solar system."}]$hdsat_report_fix$::jsonb, figure_metadata = NULL, skill = $hdsat_report_fix$Inferences$hdsat_report_fix$, domain = $hdsat_report_fix$Information and Ideas$hdsat_report_fix$, difficulty = $hdsat_report_fix$Hard$hdsat_report_fix$, format = $hdsat_report_fix$multiple_choice$hdsat_report_fix$, assessments = ARRAY[$hdsat_report_fix$SAT$hdsat_report_fix$, $hdsat_report_fix$PSAT/NMSQT$hdsat_report_fix$, $hdsat_report_fix$PSAT 10$hdsat_report_fix$, $hdsat_report_fix$PSAT 8/9$hdsat_report_fix$]::TEXT[], updated_at = NOW() WHERE id = $hdsat_report_fix$RW-M16-INF010$hdsat_report_fix$;
UPDATE public.question_keys SET correct_answer = $hdsat_report_fix$A$hdsat_report_fix$, acceptable_answers = ARRAY[$hdsat_report_fix$A$hdsat_report_fix$]::TEXT[], explanation = $hdsat_report_fix$The text places the nearest stellar approach about 40,000 years away, which supports the inference that the records are unlikely to be found and interpreted in the near future.$hdsat_report_fix$, distractor_rationales = $hdsat_report_fix${"D":"The text does not question whether the records will survive.","C":"The text states that Voyager 1 will pass near Gliese 445, so this is not a matter of likelihood.","B":"The text states that the records do contain such information."}$hdsat_report_fix$::jsonb, updated_at = NOW() WHERE question_id = $hdsat_report_fix$RW-M16-INF010$hdsat_report_fix$;
UPDATE public.questions SET stimulus = $hdsat_report_fix$Standard time is the synchronisation of clocks within a geographical region to a single time standard, rather than different localities in the region using their own local mean time. The term is also used to contrast with daylight saving time, a period of the year when clocks are shifted ahead one hour, supposedly to make better use of daily sunlight from spring to fall. Applied globally in the 20th century, the geographical regions became time zones. The standard time in each time zone has come to be defined as an offset from Universal Time. A further offset is applied for part of the year in regions with daylight saving time. Generally, standard time agrees with the local mean time at some meridian that passes through the region, often near the centre of the region. Historically, standard time was established during the 19th century to aid weather forecasting and train travel.

______$hdsat_report_fix$, prompt = $hdsat_report_fix$Which choice most logically completes the text?$hdsat_report_fix$, choices = $hdsat_report_fix$[{"id":"A","text":"Every region already used Universal Time."},{"id":"B","text":"Trains crossing a region might have kept clocks that disagreed with one another."},{"id":"C","text":"Rail travel was unaffected by local time."},{"id":"D","text":"Weather forecasting was impossible."}]$hdsat_report_fix$::jsonb, figure_metadata = NULL, skill = $hdsat_report_fix$Inferences$hdsat_report_fix$, domain = $hdsat_report_fix$Information and Ideas$hdsat_report_fix$, difficulty = $hdsat_report_fix$Hard$hdsat_report_fix$, format = $hdsat_report_fix$multiple_choice$hdsat_report_fix$, assessments = ARRAY[$hdsat_report_fix$SAT$hdsat_report_fix$, $hdsat_report_fix$PSAT/NMSQT$hdsat_report_fix$, $hdsat_report_fix$PSAT 10$hdsat_report_fix$, $hdsat_report_fix$PSAT 8/9$hdsat_report_fix$]::TEXT[], updated_at = NOW() WHERE id = $hdsat_report_fix$RW-M16-INF011$hdsat_report_fix$;
UPDATE public.question_keys SET correct_answer = $hdsat_report_fix$B$hdsat_report_fix$, acceptable_answers = ARRAY[$hdsat_report_fix$B$hdsat_report_fix$]::TEXT[], explanation = $hdsat_report_fix$Before standard time, localities used their own local mean time. The text says standard time was established to aid train travel, which supports the inference that differing local clocks caused problems for railways.$hdsat_report_fix$, distractor_rationales = $hdsat_report_fix${"D":"The text says standard time aided forecasting, not that forecasting was impossible without it.","C":"The text presents train travel as a reason for adopting standard time, which implies local time did affect it.","A":"Universal Time is described as the reference for modern time zones, not as a pre-existing standard everywhere."}$hdsat_report_fix$::jsonb, updated_at = NOW() WHERE question_id = $hdsat_report_fix$RW-M16-INF011$hdsat_report_fix$;
UPDATE public.questions SET stimulus = $hdsat_report_fix$A peatland is a type of wetland whose soils consist of organic matter from decaying plants, forming layers of peat. Peatlands arise because of incomplete decomposition of organic matter, usually litter from vegetation, due to water-logging and subsequent anoxia. Peatlands are unusual landforms that derive mostly from biological rather than physical processes, and can take on characteristic shapes and surface patterning.
The formation of peatlands is primarily controlled by climatic conditions such as precipitation and temperature, although terrain relief is a major factor as waterlogging occurs more easily on flatter ground and in basins. Peat formation typically initiates as a paludification of a mineral soil forest, terrestrialisation of lakes, or primary peat formation on bare soils on previously glaciated areas. A peatland that is actively forming peat is called a mire. All types of mires share the common characteristic of being saturated with water, at least seasonally with actively forming peat, while having their own ecosystem.
Peatlands are the largest natural carbon store on land.

______$hdsat_report_fix$, prompt = $hdsat_report_fix$Which choice most logically completes the text?$hdsat_report_fix$, choices = $hdsat_report_fix$[{"id":"A","text":"Increase the rate at which new peat forms."},{"id":"B","text":"Have no effect on the carbon stored in the peat."},{"id":"C","text":"Turn the peatland into a mire."},{"id":"D","text":"Allow stored organic matter to decompose more quickly."}]$hdsat_report_fix$::jsonb, figure_metadata = NULL, skill = $hdsat_report_fix$Inferences$hdsat_report_fix$, domain = $hdsat_report_fix$Information and Ideas$hdsat_report_fix$, difficulty = $hdsat_report_fix$Hard$hdsat_report_fix$, format = $hdsat_report_fix$multiple_choice$hdsat_report_fix$, assessments = ARRAY[$hdsat_report_fix$SAT$hdsat_report_fix$, $hdsat_report_fix$PSAT/NMSQT$hdsat_report_fix$, $hdsat_report_fix$PSAT 10$hdsat_report_fix$, $hdsat_report_fix$PSAT 8/9$hdsat_report_fix$]::TEXT[], updated_at = NOW() WHERE id = $hdsat_report_fix$RW-M16-INF012$hdsat_report_fix$;
UPDATE public.question_keys SET correct_answer = $hdsat_report_fix$D$hdsat_report_fix$, acceptable_answers = ARRAY[$hdsat_report_fix$D$hdsat_report_fix$]::TEXT[], explanation = $hdsat_report_fix$Peat accumulates because waterlogging and anoxia inhibit decomposition. Draining removes that inhibition, so the stored organic matter would break down faster.$hdsat_report_fix$, distractor_rationales = $hdsat_report_fix${"A":"Waterlogging is what slows decomposition, so removing it would work against further peat formation.","B":"The text ties carbon storage to waterlogged, oxygen-poor conditions, so changing those conditions would affect the stored carbon.","C":"A mire is a peatland that is actively forming peat, which draining would not promote."}$hdsat_report_fix$::jsonb, updated_at = NOW() WHERE question_id = $hdsat_report_fix$RW-M16-INF012$hdsat_report_fix$;
UPDATE public.questions SET stimulus = $hdsat_report_fix$Text 1
Nuclear power is the use of nuclear reactions to produce electricity. Nuclear power can be obtained from nuclear fission, nuclear decay and nuclear fusion reactions. Presently, the vast majority of electricity from nuclear power is produced by nuclear fission of uranium and plutonium in nuclear power plants. Nuclear decay processes are used in niche applications such as radioisotope thermoelectric generators in some space probes such as Voyager 2. Reactors producing controlled fusion power have been operated since 1958 but have yet to generate net power and are not expected to be commercially available in the near future.
The first nuclear power plant was built in the 1950s. The global installed nuclear capacity grew to 100 GW in the late 1970s, and then expanded during the 1980s, reaching 300 GW by 1990. The 1979 Three Mile Island accident in the United States and the 1986 Chernobyl disaster in the Soviet Union resulted in increased regulation and public opposition to nuclear power plants.

Text 2
On 26 April 1986, reactor 4 of the Chernobyl Nuclear Power Plant, located near Pripyat in the Ukrainian SSR of the Soviet Union, exploded. The accident resulted in dozens of direct deaths and a major release of radioactive material into the environment, causing widespread health effects and requiring the establishment of the Chernobyl exclusion zone. The response involved more than 500,000 personnel and cost an estimated 18 billion rubles (about \$85 billion in 2026). It remains the most destructive nuclear disaster and the most expensive disaster in history, with an estimated cost of US\$700 billion. It is one of only two nuclear accidents rated at the maximum severity on the International Nuclear Event Scale, the other being the 2011 Fukushima nuclear accident in Japan.
The disaster occurred while conducting a test to simulate the cooling of the reactor during a serious accident in blackout conditions. The operators carried out the test following an accidental drop in reactor power. Upon shutting down the reactor in an unstable condition, underlying design flaws led to a huge uncontrollable power surge.$hdsat_report_fix$, prompt = $hdsat_report_fix$Which choice best describes the relationship between the two texts?$hdsat_report_fix$, choices = $hdsat_report_fix$[{"id":"A","text":"Text 2 argues that nuclear power should replace fossil fuels, while Text 1 describes the risks of doing so."},{"id":"B","text":"Text 1 claims that accidents like Chernobyl were impossible to prevent, while Text 2 explains the safety measures that prevented them."},{"id":"C","text":"Text 1 traces the growth of nuclear power and notes, while Text 2 describes the human and financial scale of one such accident."},{"id":"D","text":"Both texts focus on the technical process by which nuclear reactors generate electricity."}]$hdsat_report_fix$::jsonb, figure_metadata = NULL, skill = $hdsat_report_fix$Cross-Text Connections$hdsat_report_fix$, domain = $hdsat_report_fix$Craft and Structure$hdsat_report_fix$, difficulty = $hdsat_report_fix$Medium$hdsat_report_fix$, format = $hdsat_report_fix$multiple_choice$hdsat_report_fix$, assessments = ARRAY[$hdsat_report_fix$SAT$hdsat_report_fix$, $hdsat_report_fix$PSAT/NMSQT$hdsat_report_fix$, $hdsat_report_fix$PSAT 10$hdsat_report_fix$, $hdsat_report_fix$PSAT 8/9$hdsat_report_fix$]::TEXT[], updated_at = NOW() WHERE id = $hdsat_report_fix$RW-M17-CT001$hdsat_report_fix$;
UPDATE public.question_keys SET correct_answer = $hdsat_report_fix$C$hdsat_report_fix$, acceptable_answers = ARRAY[$hdsat_report_fix$C$hdsat_report_fix$]::TEXT[], explanation = $hdsat_report_fix$Text 1 is a broad account of nuclear power's expansion that closes by linking two accidents to regulation and public opposition. Text 2 narrows to Chernobyl and measures its casualties, response effort and cost, illustrating the kind of event Text 1 gestures toward.$hdsat_report_fix$, distractor_rationales = $hdsat_report_fix${"A":"Text 2 never advocates replacing fossil fuels; it recounts the costs of the Chernobyl accident.","D":"Text 1 mentions fission only in passing, and neither text explains how a reactor operates.","B":"Text 1 does not claim such accidents were impossible to prevent, and Text 2 says design flaws led to an uncontrollable power surge rather than describing measures that prevented one."}$hdsat_report_fix$::jsonb, updated_at = NOW() WHERE question_id = $hdsat_report_fix$RW-M17-CT001$hdsat_report_fix$;
