UPDATE public.questions SET stimulus = $hdsat_report_fix$Remote work is the practice of working at or from one's home or another space rather than from an office or workplace.
The practice of working at home has been documented for centuries, but remote work for large employers began on a small scale in the 1970s when technology was developed that could link satellite offices to downtown mainframes through dumb terminals using telephone lines as a network bridge. It became more common in the 1990s and 2000s, facilitated by internet technologies such as collaborative software on cloud computing and conference calling via videotelephony. In 2020, workplace hazard controls during the COVID-19 pandemic catalyzed a rapid transition to remote work for white-collar workers around the world. This shift largely persisted even after restrictions were lifted.
Proponents of a geographically distributed workforce argue that it reduces costs associated with maintaining an office, grants employees greater autonomy and flexibility—which improves their motivation and job satisfaction—eliminates environmental harms from commuting, allows employers to access a more geographically diverse pool of applicants, and enables employees to live where they prefer.
Opponents of remote work argue that remote telecommunications technology has been unable to fully replicate the advantages of face-to-face interaction, that employees may be more easily distracted, and that reduced social interaction may lead to feelings of isolation.

______$hdsat_report_fix$, prompt = $hdsat_report_fix$Which choice most logically completes the text?$hdsat_report_fix$, choices = $hdsat_report_fix$[{"id":"A","text":"Found that remote technology fully replicated face-to-face interaction."},{"id":"B","text":"Abandoned remote work entirely."},{"id":"C","text":"Continued to operate with employees working outside the office."},{"id":"D","text":"Stopped hiring employees who lived far away."}]$hdsat_report_fix$::jsonb, figure_metadata = NULL, skill = $hdsat_report_fix$Inferences$hdsat_report_fix$, domain = $hdsat_report_fix$Information and Ideas$hdsat_report_fix$, difficulty = $hdsat_report_fix$Medium$hdsat_report_fix$, format = $hdsat_report_fix$multiple_choice$hdsat_report_fix$, assessments = ARRAY[$hdsat_report_fix$SAT$hdsat_report_fix$, $hdsat_report_fix$PSAT/NMSQT$hdsat_report_fix$, $hdsat_report_fix$PSAT 10$hdsat_report_fix$, $hdsat_report_fix$PSAT 8/9$hdsat_report_fix$]::TEXT[], updated_at = NOW() WHERE id = $hdsat_report_fix$RW-M17-INF002$hdsat_report_fix$;
UPDATE public.question_keys SET correct_answer = $hdsat_report_fix$C$hdsat_report_fix$, acceptable_answers = ARRAY[$hdsat_report_fix$C$hdsat_report_fix$]::TEXT[], explanation = $hdsat_report_fix$Persistence of the shift means remote arrangements continued after restrictions ended. The passage supplies no support for the other outcomes.$hdsat_report_fix$, distractor_rationales = $hdsat_report_fix${"B":"The text says the shift largely persisted, which rules out abandoning it.","A":"The text attributes the opposite view to opponents, who say technology has not fully replicated face-to-face interaction.","D":"Proponents argue that remote work lets employers reach a more geographically diverse pool of applicants, not a narrower one."}$hdsat_report_fix$::jsonb, updated_at = NOW() WHERE question_id = $hdsat_report_fix$RW-M17-INF002$hdsat_report_fix$;
UPDATE public.questions SET stimulus = $hdsat_report_fix$A standardized test is a test that is administered and scored in a consistent or standard manner. Standardized tests are designed in such a way that the questions and interpretations are consistent and are administered and scored in a predetermined, standard manner.
A standardized test is administered and scored uniformly for all test takers. Any test in which the same test is given in the same manner to all test takers, and graded in the same manner for everyone, is a standardized test. Standardized tests do not need to be high-stakes tests, time-limited tests, multiple-choice tests, academic tests, or tests given to large numbers of test takers. Standardized tests can take various forms, including written, oral, or practical test. The standardized test may evaluate many subjects, including driving, creativity, athleticism, personality, professional ethics, as well as academic skills.
The opposite of standardized testing is non-standardized testing, in which either significantly different tests are given to different test takers, or the same test is assigned under significantly different conditions or evaluated differently.

______$hdsat_report_fix$, prompt = $hdsat_report_fix$Which choice most logically completes the text?$hdsat_report_fix$, choices = $hdsat_report_fix$[{"id":"A","text":"Would be an example of standardized administration but standardized scoring."},{"id":"B","text":"Would not qualify as standardized."},{"id":"C","text":"Would still be standardized if it were given at the same time."},{"id":"D","text":"Would be standardized as long as its questions were multiple-choice."}]$hdsat_report_fix$::jsonb, figure_metadata = NULL, skill = $hdsat_report_fix$Inferences$hdsat_report_fix$, domain = $hdsat_report_fix$Information and Ideas$hdsat_report_fix$, difficulty = $hdsat_report_fix$Easy$hdsat_report_fix$, format = $hdsat_report_fix$multiple_choice$hdsat_report_fix$, assessments = ARRAY[$hdsat_report_fix$SAT$hdsat_report_fix$, $hdsat_report_fix$PSAT/NMSQT$hdsat_report_fix$, $hdsat_report_fix$PSAT 10$hdsat_report_fix$, $hdsat_report_fix$PSAT 8/9$hdsat_report_fix$]::TEXT[], updated_at = NOW() WHERE id = $hdsat_report_fix$RW-M17-INF003$hdsat_report_fix$;
UPDATE public.question_keys SET correct_answer = $hdsat_report_fix$B$hdsat_report_fix$, acceptable_answers = ARRAY[$hdsat_report_fix$B$hdsat_report_fix$]::TEXT[], explanation = $hdsat_report_fix$The passage defines the opposite of standardized testing as including the same test evaluated differently. Grading essays by different standards is exactly that case.$hdsat_report_fix$, distractor_rationales = $hdsat_report_fix${"C":"Uniform timing does not make up for scoring that differs across students.","A":"The choice contradicts itself and ignores the differing standards.","D":"The text says a test need not be multiple-choice to be standardized, so format cannot rescue inconsistent scoring."}$hdsat_report_fix$::jsonb, updated_at = NOW() WHERE question_id = $hdsat_report_fix$RW-M17-INF003$hdsat_report_fix$;
UPDATE public.questions SET stimulus = $hdsat_report_fix$An electric vehicle (EV) is a vehicle propelled mostly by electric power. EVs encompass electric cars and other electric road vehicles (buses, trucks and personal transporters) as well as other types of land vehicles, such as electric rail vehicles (trains, trams and monorails). It also includes electric watercraft (boats and submersibles), aircraft (fixed-wing and multirotors), and spacecraft.
EVs originated in the late 19th century. Electricity was among the preferred methods for powering early motor vehicles because it was quieter, provided comfort, and ease of operation. However, its limited range hindered mass adoption throughout the 20th century. Internal combustion engines were the dominant propulsion mechanisms for cars and trucks for about 100 years, although electricity-powered locomotion became commonplace in other vehicle types, such as overhead line-powered mass transit vehicles, as well as special purpose vehicles such as mobility scooters.
Since the late 20th century, technological advances in lithium batteries—which offer superior energy density and current output versus lead-acid batteries—have revived public interest as zero-emission vehicle options.

______$hdsat_report_fix$, prompt = $hdsat_report_fix$Which choice most logically completes the text?$hdsat_report_fix$, choices = $hdsat_report_fix$[{"id":"A","text":"Quickly displaced internal combustion engines."},{"id":"B","text":"Were preferred over other vehicles for their high speed."},{"id":"C","text":"Were not widely adopted even though they were quiet and easy to operate."},{"id":"D","text":"Were used mainly for long-distance travel."}]$hdsat_report_fix$::jsonb, figure_metadata = NULL, skill = $hdsat_report_fix$Inferences$hdsat_report_fix$, domain = $hdsat_report_fix$Information and Ideas$hdsat_report_fix$, difficulty = $hdsat_report_fix$Easy$hdsat_report_fix$, format = $hdsat_report_fix$multiple_choice$hdsat_report_fix$, assessments = ARRAY[$hdsat_report_fix$SAT$hdsat_report_fix$, $hdsat_report_fix$PSAT/NMSQT$hdsat_report_fix$, $hdsat_report_fix$PSAT 10$hdsat_report_fix$, $hdsat_report_fix$PSAT 8/9$hdsat_report_fix$]::TEXT[], updated_at = NOW() WHERE id = $hdsat_report_fix$RW-M17-INF004$hdsat_report_fix$;
UPDATE public.question_keys SET correct_answer = $hdsat_report_fix$C$hdsat_report_fix$, acceptable_answers = ARRAY[$hdsat_report_fix$C$hdsat_report_fix$]::TEXT[], explanation = $hdsat_report_fix$The passage presents limited range as the obstacle to mass adoption despite the quiet, comfortable operation that made electric power attractive.$hdsat_report_fix$, distractor_rationales = $hdsat_report_fix${"A":"The text says internal combustion engines dominated for about 100 years, so electric vehicles did not displace them.","D":"A vehicle with limited range would not be suited to long-distance travel.","B":"The text praises quietness and ease of operation, not speed."}$hdsat_report_fix$::jsonb, updated_at = NOW() WHERE question_id = $hdsat_report_fix$RW-M17-INF004$hdsat_report_fix$;
UPDATE public.questions SET stimulus = $hdsat_report_fix$Urban sprawl (also known as suburban sprawl or urban encroachment) is defined as "the rapid expansion of the geographic extent of cities and towns, often characterized by low-density residential housing, single-use zoning, and increased reliance on the private automobile for transportation". Urban sprawl has been described as the unrestricted spread over large expanses of land around an urban area of housing, commercial development, and roads, with little concern for very dense urban planning.
Urban sprawl refers to a special form of urbanization, and it relates to the social and environmental consequences associated with such development. In modern times some suburban areas described as "sprawl" have less detached housing and higher density than the nearby core city. Medieval suburbs suffered from the loss of protection of city walls, before the advent of industrial warfare. Sometimes the urban areas described as the most "sprawling" are the most densely populated.
The modern disadvantages and costs of urban sprawl include increased travel time, transport costs, pollution, and destruction of the countryside. The revenue for building and maintaining urban infrastructure in these areas is gained mostly through property and sales taxes.

______$hdsat_report_fix$, prompt = $hdsat_report_fix$Which choice most logically completes the text?$hdsat_report_fix$, choices = $hdsat_report_fix$[{"id":"A","text":"Lowering property taxes in new suburbs."},{"id":"B","text":"Making it easier for residents to use alternatives to driving."},{"id":"C","text":"Building more roads at the edges of cities."},{"id":"D","text":"Encouraging single-use zoning."}]$hdsat_report_fix$::jsonb, figure_metadata = NULL, skill = $hdsat_report_fix$Inferences$hdsat_report_fix$, domain = $hdsat_report_fix$Information and Ideas$hdsat_report_fix$, difficulty = $hdsat_report_fix$Hard$hdsat_report_fix$, format = $hdsat_report_fix$multiple_choice$hdsat_report_fix$, assessments = ARRAY[$hdsat_report_fix$SAT$hdsat_report_fix$, $hdsat_report_fix$PSAT/NMSQT$hdsat_report_fix$, $hdsat_report_fix$PSAT 10$hdsat_report_fix$, $hdsat_report_fix$PSAT 8/9$hdsat_report_fix$]::TEXT[], updated_at = NOW() WHERE id = $hdsat_report_fix$RW-M17-INF005$hdsat_report_fix$;
UPDATE public.question_keys SET correct_answer = $hdsat_report_fix$B$hdsat_report_fix$, acceptable_answers = ARRAY[$hdsat_report_fix$B$hdsat_report_fix$]::TEXT[], explanation = $hdsat_report_fix$If car dependence produces the travel costs, reducing those costs means giving residents ways to travel without cars. The passage supports no other remedy.$hdsat_report_fix$, distractor_rationales = $hdsat_report_fix${"C":"More roads at the edges would extend the automobile-dependent pattern rather than reduce its costs.","D":"Single-use zoning is identified as a feature of sprawl, not a remedy for it.","A":"The text mentions property and sales taxes as revenue sources, not as a way to reduce travel costs."}$hdsat_report_fix$::jsonb, updated_at = NOW() WHERE question_id = $hdsat_report_fix$RW-M17-INF005$hdsat_report_fix$;
UPDATE public.questions SET stimulus = $hdsat_report_fix$A zoo (short for zoological garden; also called a zoological park, animal park, or menagerie) is a facility where animals are kept within enclosures for exhibition and often bred for conservation purposes. Zoos vary greatly in size, ranging from small urban enclosures of a few acres to massive open-range and natural-habitat reserves spanning thousands of acres.
The term zoological garden refers to zoology, the study of animals. The term is derived from the Ancient Greek ζῷον, zōion, 'animal', and the suffix -λογία, -logia, 'study of'. The abbreviation zoo was first used of the London Zoological Gardens, which was opened for scientific study in 1828, and to the public in 1847. The first modern zoo was the Tierpark Hagenbeck by Carl Hagenbeck in Germany. In the United States alone, zoos are visited by over 181 million people annually.
However, animal welfare organizations have expressed criticism of zoos and the conditions in which animals are kept.

______$hdsat_report_fix$, prompt = $hdsat_report_fix$Which choice most logically completes the text?$hdsat_report_fix$, choices = $hdsat_report_fix$[{"id":"A","text":"No longer need to breed animals for conservation."},{"id":"B","text":"Have eliminated the need for zoological study."},{"id":"C","text":"Be trying to address those criticisms."},{"id":"D","text":"Have stopped exhibiting animals to the public."}]$hdsat_report_fix$::jsonb, figure_metadata = NULL, skill = $hdsat_report_fix$Inferences$hdsat_report_fix$, domain = $hdsat_report_fix$Information and Ideas$hdsat_report_fix$, difficulty = $hdsat_report_fix$Hard$hdsat_report_fix$, format = $hdsat_report_fix$multiple_choice$hdsat_report_fix$, assessments = ARRAY[$hdsat_report_fix$SAT$hdsat_report_fix$, $hdsat_report_fix$PSAT/NMSQT$hdsat_report_fix$, $hdsat_report_fix$PSAT 10$hdsat_report_fix$, $hdsat_report_fix$PSAT 8/9$hdsat_report_fix$]::TEXT[], updated_at = NOW() WHERE id = $hdsat_report_fix$RW-M17-INF006$hdsat_report_fix$;
UPDATE public.question_keys SET correct_answer = $hdsat_report_fix$C$hdsat_report_fix$, acceptable_answers = ARRAY[$hdsat_report_fix$C$hdsat_report_fix$]::TEXT[], explanation = $hdsat_report_fix$Criticism focuses on the conditions in which animals are kept. Moving animals into large natural-habitat reserves changes those conditions in the direction the criticism points.$hdsat_report_fix$, distractor_rationales = $hdsat_report_fix${"D":"The text describes zoos as facilities where animals are kept for exhibition, and expanding enclosures does not imply ending exhibition.","A":"Conservation breeding is described as common in zoos and is not tied to enclosure size.","B":"The text does not suggest that enclosure size removes the study of animals."}$hdsat_report_fix$::jsonb, updated_at = NOW() WHERE question_id = $hdsat_report_fix$RW-M17-INF006$hdsat_report_fix$;
UPDATE public.questions SET stimulus = $hdsat_report_fix$Space exploration is the physical investigation of outer space by uncrewed robotic space probes and through human spaceflight.
While the observation of objects in space, known as astronomy, predates reliable recorded history, it was the development of large and relatively efficient rockets during the mid-twentieth century that allowed physical space exploration to become a reality. Common rationales for exploring space include advancing scientific research, national prestige, uniting different countries, ensuring the future survival of humanity, and developing military and strategic advantages against other countries.
The early era of space exploration was driven by a "Space Race" in which the Soviet Union and the United States vied to demonstrate their technological superiority. Landmarks of this era include the launch of the first human-made object to orbit Earth, the Soviet Union's Sputnik 1, on 4 October 1957, and the first Moon landing by the American Apollo 11 mission on 20 July 1969. The Soviet space program achieved many of the first milestones, including the first living being in orbit in 1957, the first human spaceflight (Yuri Gagarin aboard Vostok 1) in 1961, the first spacewalk (by Alexei Leonov) on 18 March 1965, the first automatic landing on another celestial body in 1966, and the launch of the first space station (Salyut 1) in 1971. 
In the 1970s, focus shifted from one-off flights to renewable hardware, such as the Space Shuttle program, and from competition to cooperation, the foremost example being the International Space Station (ISS), built between 1998 and 2011.
The 2000s brought advancements in the national space-exploration programs of China, the European Union, Japan, and India.

